# Every environment variable the control plane reads, with fake values.
# For local runs: copy this file to .env (gitignored), fill in the "Local"
# section, and start the app with scripts/run-local.sh, which loads .env and
# always uses the local profile. The test profile needs none of these.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Trusted ID token issuers (ADR 0009). By default scripts/run-local.sh ignores
# these two lines and trusts the development sign-in server from compose.yaml
# (ADR 0013, docs/runbooks/local-stack.md). To sign in with your own Google
# Identity Platform project, fill them in with its project id and start with
# scripts/run-local.sh --real-sign-in (docs/runbooks/identity-provider-setup.md).
# The issuer is https://securetoken.google.com/<project-id> and the audience is
# the project id. Add _1_ variables for a second issuer during a provider move.
# IDENTITY_ISSUERS_0_ISSUERURI=https://securetoken.google.com/demo-project
# IDENTITY_ISSUERS_0_AUDIENCE=demo-project

# The development sign-in server's port (compose.yaml), if 8099 is taken.
# LOCAL_SIGN_IN_PORT=8099

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production: set by the deployment, not here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deployment sets the profile and these variables in its own environment
# settings. Never put them in .env: a local run must not pick them up. The
# prod profile itself is never set in any file.
#
# DATABASE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://db.example.invalid:25060/controlplane?sslmode=require
# DATABASE_USERNAME=controlplane
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=fake-password-change-me
#
# Our certificate authority and the agents' gRPC TLS (ADR 0010,
# docs/runbooks/certificate-authority.md). Each value is PEM content or a
# file: location. Locally, scripts/dev-ca.sh stands in for all of these.
# HOSTS_CA_ROOT_CERTIFICATE=file:/run/secrets/ca-root.pem
# HOSTS_CA_INTERMEDIATE_CERTIFICATE=file:/run/secrets/ca-intermediate.pem
# HOSTS_CA_INTERMEDIATE_PRIVATE_KEY=file:/run/secrets/ca-intermediate.key
# HOSTS_CA_INTERMEDIATE_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=fake-password-change-me
#
# The key that encrypts admins' TOTP secrets (ADR 0027, docs/runbooks/admin.md):
# 32 random bytes in base64 (openssl rand -base64 32), or a file: location
# holding that. Locally, scripts/dev-ca.sh makes private/dev-ca/admin-totp.key.
# IDENTITY_ADMIN_TOTP_KEY=file:/run/secrets/admin-totp.key
# AGENT_GRPC_TLS_CERTIFICATE=file:/run/secrets/agent-grpc-chain.pem
# AGENT_GRPC_TLS_PRIVATE_KEY=file:/run/secrets/agent-grpc.key
#
# Valkey, for host presence (ADR 0011). Locally, compose.yaml runs it.
# VALKEY_URL=rediss://default:fake-password-change-me@valkey.example.invalid:25061
#
# Email (ADR 0026, docs/runbooks/email.md): any provider that speaks SMTP with STARTTLS. Locally, compose.yaml runs
# a mail catcher (Mailpit) and the local profile sends to it. The web app's address is where an email's links go.
# MAIL_HOST=smtp.example.invalid
# MAIL_PORT=587
# MAIL_USERNAME=fake-username
# MAIL_PASSWORD=fake-password-change-me
# NOTIFICATIONS_EMAIL_FROM=IdleO <notifications@example.invalid>
# NOTIFICATIONS_WEB_APP_URL=https://app.example.invalid
# The email provider's bounce and complaint webhook's signing secret (ADR 0036): Resend's whsec_… for
# POST /v1/email-events/resend. Production refuses to start without one.
# NOTIFICATIONS_EMAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_ZmFrZS1zZWNyZXQtY2hhbmdlLW1l
#
# Metrics, traces and logs through OTLP (ADR 0036, docs/runbooks/monitoring.md): Grafana Cloud's OTLP endpoint
# (without /v1/...) and the whole Authorization header ("Basic " and base64 of instance-id:token). The release,
# environment and instance name every signal.
# OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://otlp-gateway-prod-example.grafana.net/otlp
# OTLP_AUTHORIZATION=Basic ZmFrZTpmYWtlLXRva2Vu
# IDLEO_RELEASE=2026.10.20-abc1234
# IDLEO_ENVIRONMENT=production
# IDLEO_INSTANCE=control-plane-1
#
# The database's backups, as the control plane watches them (ADR 0036, docs/runbooks/backups.md): an R2 token that
# may only read the backup bucket. The database's own container has a read-write one.
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://fake-account.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_BUCKET=idleo-backups
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_PREFIX=production
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_REGION=auto
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=fake-read-only-key-id
# BACKUPS_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=fake-password-change-me
